  "nl_text": "The database schema for the database named 'rschema_0406' consists of 18 tables. The database is used for managing various aspects of a business, such as accounting, inventory management, project planning, and sales.\nTables in this schema include 'general_ledger', which records accounting transactions, 'accounts_receivable' and 'accounts_payable' which manage customer and supplier payments, 'customer_information' and 'supplier_information' which store customer and supplier details, 'purchase_order' and 'sales_order' which manage orders, 'material' which stores information about the materials used in the business, 'warehouse_information' which stores information about the warehouses, 'project_plan' which outlines the project plans, 'supplier_material_supply' which links suppliers and materials, 'material_inventory' which tracks the inventory of materials in different warehouses, 'cost_accounting' which records the cost accounting transactions, 'employee_information' and 'performance' which manage employee records and performance evaluations, 'production_plan' which outlines production plans, 'material_requirement_plan' which outlines material requirements, and 'production_scheduling' which schedules the production. The database is designed in such a way that all these tables are interconnected, with foreign keys used to link related data across tables. For instance, the 'purchase_order' table references the 'material' table through a foreign key, and the 'sales_order' table references the 'purchase_order' table through a foreign key. The database also includes cascading delete and update rules, which automatically delete or update related records when the primary key of a record in a table is deleted or updated. This ensures data integrity and consistency in the database.",
  "gold_question": "The following is a database requirement description for an enterprise resource planning (ERP) system:\n\nThe ERP system covers multiple modules, including financial management, human resource management, production management, sales management, procurement management, inventory management, and project management. Each module has related entities, and these entities are associated through relationships to ensure data consistency and collaboration.\n\nIn the financial management module, the system records accounting information of various company accounts through the general ledger, including Attributes such as general ledger ID, account name, amount, and date. At the same time, accounts receivable records customer receivable information, such as accounts receivable ID, customer ID, amount, and due date. Accounts payable records the amounts the company owes to suppliers, including accounts payable ID, supplier ID, amount, and due date. Cost accounting records expense information of cost centers, including cost accounting ID, cost center, amount, and date.\n\nIn the human resource management module, the employee information table manages basic information of all employees, such as employee ID, name, position, hire date, and salary. Meanwhile, the performance table records employee performance evaluation information, generating performance scores based on employee ID and evaluation date.\n\nThe production management module includes production planning, material requirements planning, and production scheduling. The production plan records detailed information of each production plan, such as plan ID, product name, production quantity, start and end dates. The material requirements plan records the required quantity and date of materials based on production plan requirements. The production scheduling table is used to manage scheduling information during the production process, including scheduling ID, production plan ID, production date, and scheduling status.\n\nIn the sales management module, the sales order table records customer order information, including order ID, customer ID, order date, and total amount. At the same time, customer information records basic customer data, such as customer ID, name, contact information, and address.\n\nIn the procurement management module, the purchase order records supplier order information, including order ID, supplier ID, order date, and total amount. Supplier information manages basic supplier information, such as supplier ID, name, contact information, and address.\n\nThe inventory management module contains material and warehouse information. The material table records basic information of all materials, such as material ID, name, description, and type. Warehouse information manages information of each warehouse, including warehouse ID, warehouse name, and address.\n\nIn the project management module, the project plan table records detailed project information, including project ID, name, planned start and end dates, execution status, and project progress.\n\nAdditionally, the system ensures association and collaboration between modules through relationship tables. For example, the supplier material supply relationship table ensures the many-to-many relationship between suppliers and materials, while the material inventory table records inventory information between materials and warehouses.\n\nThrough these detailed entity and relationship designs, the system ensures data accuracy and effectiveness of each module, supporting efficient operation of various enterprise management functions.",
  "input_json": "/data/RSchema_ExpResults4/Experiment2/schema2text_deepseek/0406.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: rschema_0406\nCREATE TABLE `general_ledger` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `transaction_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `amount` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `description` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `accounts_receivable` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `customer_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `amount_due` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `due_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `accounts_receivable_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`customer_id`) REFERENCES `customer_information` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `accounts_payable` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `supplier_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `amount_due` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `due_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `accounts_payable_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`supplier_id`) REFERENCES `supplier_information` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `customer_information` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `contact_info` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `supplier_information` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `contact_info` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `purchase_order` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `supplier_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `material_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `order_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `purchase_order_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`supplier_id`) REFERENCES `supplier_information` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `purchase_order_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`material_id`) REFERENCES `material` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `sales_order` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `customer_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `purchase_order_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `order_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `sales_order_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`customer_id`) REFERENCES `customer_information` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `sales_order_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`purchase_order_id`) REFERENCES `purchase_order` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `material` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `description` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `warehouse_information` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `location` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `capacity` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `project_plan` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `project_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `start_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `end_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `supplier_material_supply` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `supplier_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `material_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `supplier_material_supply_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`supplier_id`) REFERENCES `supplier_information` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `supplier_material_supply_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`material_id`) REFERENCES `material` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `material_inventory` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `material_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `warehouse_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `quantity` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `material_inventory_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`material_id`) REFERENCES `material` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `material_inventory_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`warehouse_id`) REFERENCES `warehouse_information` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `cost_accounting` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `transaction_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `amount` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `description` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `employee_information` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `position` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `performance` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `employee_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `evaluation_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `score` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `performance_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`employee_id`) REFERENCES `employee_information` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `production_plan` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `plan_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `start_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `end_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `material_requirement_plan` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `material_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `required_quantity` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `material_requirement_plan_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`material_id`) REFERENCES `material` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `production_scheduling` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `production_plan_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `schedule_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `production_scheduling_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`production_plan_id`) REFERENCES `production_plan` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
